  "response": "# Phase AGIR — Itération 1\n\n## Analyse\n\n**Tâche** : Créer `tools/summarize.py` avec une fonction `summarize(text, max_sentences=3)` qui extrait les phrases les plus importantes.\n\n**Approche** :\n- Heuristique simple : score = (longueur_phrase + présence_mots_clés)\n- Mots-clés = mots fréquents (> 1 occurrence, non-stopwords)\n- Extraction des N phrases avec meilleur score\n- Préservation de l'ordre original\n- Test dans `if __name__ == '__main__'`\n\n**Critères d'acceptation** :\n✓ Fichier existe à `tools/summarize.py`\n✓ Fonction importable\n✓ Retourne un résumé plus court que l'original\n\n---\n\n## Fichiers à créer/modifier\n\n```path: tools/__init__.py\n\"\"\"Tools package for MAEL.\"\"\"\n```\n\n```path: tools/summarize.py\n\"\"\"Text summarization utility using simple heuristics.\"\"\"\n\nimport re\nfrom typing import List, Tuple\n\n\n# Common English stopwords\nSTOPWORDS = {\n    'a', 'an', 'and', 'are', 'as', 'at', 'be', 'by', 'for', 'from',\n    'has', 'he', 'in', 'is', 'it', 'its', 'of', 'on', 'or', 'that',\n    'the', 'to', 'was', 'will', 'with', 'i', 'you', 'we', 'they',\n    'this', 'which', 'who', 'what', 'when', 'where', 'why', 'how'\n}\n\n\ndef _extract_sentences(text: str) -> List[str]:\n    \"\"\"Split text into sentences.\"\"\"\n    # Simple sentence splitting on . ! ?\n    sentences = re.split(r'(?<=[.!?])\\s+', text.strip())\n    return [s.strip() for s in sentences if s.strip()]\n\n\ndef _extract_keywords(text: str) -> set:\n    \"\"\"Extract keywords (frequent non-stopword tokens).\"\"\"\n    # Tokenize: lowercase, remove punctuation\n    words = re.findall(r'\\b\\w+\\b', text.lower())\n    \n    # Count word frequencies\n    word_freq = {}\n    for word in words:\n        if word not in STOPWORDS:\n            word_freq[word] = word_freq.get(word, 0) + 1\n    \n    # Keywords = words appearing more than once\n    keywords = {word for word, freq in word_freq.items() if freq > 1}\n    return keywords\n\n\ndef _score_sentence(sentence: str, keywords: set) -> float:\n    \"\"\"Score a sentence based on length and keyword presence.\"\"\"\n    # Normalize: lowercase\n    sentence_lower = sentence.lower()\n    \n    # Length score: prefer medium-length sentences (5-20 words)\n    words = re.findall(r'\\b\\w+\\b', sentence_lower)\n    word_count = len(words)\n    length_score = min(word_count / 15.0, 1.0)  # Normalize to [0, 1]\n    \n    # Keyword score: fraction of words that are keywords\n    keyword_count = sum(1 for word in words if word in keywords)\n    keyword_score = keyword_count / max(word_count, 1)\n    \n    # Combined score (weighted average)\n    score = 0.6 * length_score + 0.4 * keyword_score\n    return score\n\n\ndef summarize(text: str, max_sentences: int = 3) -> str:\n    \"\"\"\n    Summarize text by extracting the most important sentences.\n    \n    Args:\n        text: Input text to summarize\n        max_sentences: Maximum number of sentences in summary (default: 3)\n    \n    Returns:\n        Summarized text (subset of original sentences in original order)\n    \"\"\"\n    sentences = _extract_sentences(text)\n    \n    # Handle edge cases\n    if len(sentences) <= max_sentences:\n        return text\n    \n    if not sentences:\n        return \"\"\n    \n    # Extract keywords from full text\n    keywords = _extract_keywords(text)\n    \n    # Score each sentence\n    scored_sentences: List[Tuple[int, str, float]] = []\n    for idx, sentence in enumerate(sentences):\n        score = _score_sentence(sentence, keywords)\n        scored_sentences.append((idx, sentence, score))\n    \n    # Select top max_sentences by score\n    top_sentences = sorted(scored_sentences, key=lambda x: x[2], reverse=True)[:max_sentences]\n    \n    # Restore original order\n    top_sentences.sort(key=lambda x: x[0])\n    \n    # Reconstruct summary\n    summary = ' '.join([s[1] for s in top_sentences])\n    return summary\n\n\nif __name__ == '__main__':\n    # Test example\n    test_text = \"\"\"\n    Artificial intelligence is transforming industries worldwide. \n    Machine learning algorithms can process vast amounts of data. \n    Deep learning models are particularly effective for image recognition. \n    Natural language processing enables computers to understand human language. \n    These technologies are becoming increasingly important in modern society.\n    \"\"\"\n    \n    print(\"Original text:\")\n    print(test_text.strip())\n    print(\"\\n\" + \"=\"*60 + \"\\n\")\n    \n    summary = summarize(test_text, max_sentences=3)\n    print(\"Summary (3 sentences):\")\n    print(summary)\n    print(\"\\n\" + \"=\"*60 + \"\\n\")\n    \n    # Verify criteria\n    original_length = len(test_text.split())\n    summary_length = len(summary.split())\n    print(f\"Original word count: {original_length}\")\n    print(f\"Summary word count: {summary_length}\")\n    print(f\"Reduction: {100 * (1 - summary_length/original_length):.1f}%\")\n    print(f\"✓ Summary is shorter: {summary_length < original_length}\")\n```\n\n---\n\n## Commande de validation\n\n```bash\n# Test que le fichier existe et la fonction est importable\npython -c \"from tools.summarize import summarize; print('✓ Import successful')\"\n\n# Exécuter le test intégré\npython tools/summarize.py\n\n# Vérifier que le résumé est plus court\npython -c \"\nfrom tools.summarize import summarize\ntext = 'This is sentence one. This is sentence two. This is sentence three. This is sentence four. This is sentence five.'\nsummary = summarize(text, max_sentences=2)\nassert len(summary.split()) < len(text.split()), 'Summary must be shorter'\nprint('✓ All criteria met')\n\"\n```\n\n---\n\n## Résumé de l'implémentation\n\n| Critère | Statut |\n|---------|--------|\n| Fichier `tools/summarize.py` créé | ✓ |\n| Fonction `summarize(text, max_sentences=3)` | ✓ |\n| Heuristique (longueur + mots-clés) | ✓ |\n| Retourne résumé plus court | ✓ |\n| `if __name__ == '__main__'` avec test | ✓ |\n| Importable |